## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** Countable Choice, an open set $\Omega\subseteq\mathbb R^n$, and $u\in BV(\Omega)$.

[A1] Countable Choice is used by the Lebesgue regularity and approximate-identity suppliers below. ([[def-countable-choice]])

[F1] Under Countable Choice, Lebesgue measure on $\mathbb R^n$ is Radon and regular, with finite measure on bounded sets. ([[thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-radon-measure-on-rn]])

[F2] A unit-mass smooth bump generates a nonnegative $L^1$ approximate identity by rescaling. ([[def-l-one-approximate-identity-on-rn]], [[def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump]], [[prop-mollifier-families-are-l-one-approximate-identities]], [[thm-l-one-approximate-identities-converge-in-l-p]])

[F3] Convolution of a locally integrable function with a mollifier is smooth and differentiates under the integral sign. ([[thm-convolution-with-a-mollifier-is-smooth-and-differentiates-under-the-integral-sign]])

[F4] For nonnegative product-measurable functions on sigma-finite spaces, product and iterated integrals agree. ([[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]])

[F5] The distributional variation equals the test-field supremum and is lower semicontinuous under $L^1$ convergence. ([[lem-ip-bv-test-field-characterization-and-lsc]])

[F6] Every open Euclidean set has a locally finite nonnegative smooth partition of unity with compact supports. ([[lem-ip-open-euclidean-locally-finite-smooth-partition]])

[F7] Smooth cutoffs exist on nested balls, including cutoffs equal to one on a smaller ball. ([[lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls]])

## Proof

**Given:** Countable Choice, $\Omega$, and $u$ as stated.

1.1 By [F6], take a countable locally finite nonnegative smooth partition of unity $(\eta_j)$ on $\Omega$, with each $\eta_j$ compactly supported in $\Omega$. [F6, given, choose]

2.1 Extend each $w_j:=\eta_j u$ by zero to $\mathbb R^n$. Fix a nonnegative unit-mass smooth bump $\rho$ and its rescalings $\rho_\varepsilon$. For each $j$, the approximate-identity property [F2] gives $w_j*\rho_\varepsilon\to w_j$ in $L^1$, and also $(u\nabla\eta_j)*\rho_\varepsilon\to u\nabla\eta_j$ in $L^1$. Choose the least nonnegative integer $m_j$ for which $\varepsilon_j:=2^{-m_j}$ is small enough that $\varepsilon_j<2^{-j}$, the convolution supports remain in $\Omega$, and $\|w_j*\rho_{\varepsilon_j}-w_j\|_1+\|(u\nabla\eta_j)*\rho_{\varepsilon_j}-u\nabla\eta_j\|_1<2^{-j}\delta$. Set $u_\delta:=\sum_j w_j*\rho_{\varepsilon_j}$. This sum is locally finite: for a compact $C\Subset\Omega$, choose $r>0$ so its closed $r$-neighborhood $C_r$ is compactly contained in $\Omega$. For all sufficiently large $j$, $\varepsilon_j<r$; if the thickened support of $w_j$ meets $C$, then $\operatorname{supp}\eta_j$ meets $C_r$, and local finiteness of $(\eta_j)$ leaves only finitely many such indices. Thus $u_\delta$ is smooth by [F3]. Summing the first error terms and using $\sum_j\eta_j=1$ gives $\|u_\delta-u\|_{L^1(\Omega)}<\delta$. [A1, F2, F3, F6, step 1.1, choose, construct]

3.1 The product rule follows by testing against $C_c^\infty$: $D(\eta_j u)=\eta_jDu+u\nabla\eta_j\,dx$. Convolution commutes with distributional derivatives by the defining test pairing and Fubini [F4]. Since $\sum_j\eta_j=1$, local finiteness gives $\sum_j u\nabla\eta_j=0$. Therefore $\nabla u_\delta=\sum_j (\eta_jDu)*\rho_{\varepsilon_j}+\sum_j\big((u\nabla\eta_j)*\rho_{\varepsilon_j}-u\nabla\eta_j\big)$. For the first sum, $| (\eta_jDu)*\rho_{\varepsilon_j}|\le(\eta_j|Du|)*\rho_{\varepsilon_j}$ pointwise, so Tonelli and $\sum_j\eta_j=1$ bound its $L^1$ norm by $|Du|(\Omega)$. The second sum has $L^1$ norm below $\delta$ by the choices in step 2.1. Hence $\int_\Omega|\nabla u_\delta|\,dx\le TV(u;\Omega)+\delta$. [F1, F4, step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 Take $\delta_k=1/k$ and set $u_k=u_{\delta_k}$. Step 2.1 gives $u_k\to u$ in $L^1$, while step 3.1 gives $\limsup_k\int_\Omega|\nabla u_k|\,dx\le TV(u;\Omega)$. The choice-free lower-semicontinuity clause of [F5], applied to $u_k,u\in BV(\Omega)$, gives the reverse inequality. Thus the variations converge. For $\Omega=\mathbb R^n$, choose a smooth cutoff $\chi_R$ equal to one on $B(0,R)$, supported in $B(0,2R)$, and satisfying $|\nabla\chi_R|\le C/R$, using [F7]. Since each $u_{1/(2k)}$ and its gradient are integrable, choose $R_k$ so its $L^1$ tail, gradient tail, and $C\|u_{1/(2k)}\|_1/R_k$ are all below $1/k$. The products $\chi_{R_k}u_{1/(2k)}$ are compactly supported and smooth, converge to $u$ in $L^1$, and have gradient integrals bounded above by $TV(u;\mathbb R^n)+O(1/k)$; lower semicontinuity gives convergence of their variations as well. [A1, F5, F7, step 2.1, step 3.1, choose, construct] ∎ 